      "title": "Dispatch-word publication: peer stores precede the barrier",
      "answer": "The affected four-byte shared word tells each worker warp which role to run, then tells it to exit. Threads 0–127 all publish that word with an ordinary `st.shared.b32`; the following `barrier.sync 1` coordinates them with worker readers, but does not order the peer stores before the barrier. This remains relevant in the supplied **zero-tile configuration**: setup and exit still publish the word even though the matrix-work loops are skipped.\n\nThe source establishes this publication pattern at startup and exit. The saved pair has no supplied event-to-PTX or actor-to-thread binding that selects either site. The example below therefore uses two independently derived writer threads; it does not identify the retained pair. Both write the same literal value, which creates no ordering edge. The saved duplicate-TMA-store rule applies to TMA stores, whereas these instructions are ordinary shared-memory stores.",
